# Changes: re-authored, and the premise corrected. `150` answers every locale with one pinned
#          US-QWERTY map -- the task's own audit called that out (6.5): "returns one pinned
#          US-QWERTY map, so 'follows the claimed locale' is still authoring". What Veil takes is
#          the hook point and the problem statement; the per-locale tables, the locale selection
#          and the absent-key erase are Veil's. `150` also rewrites the map from the persona struct
#          (6.9: not taken -- one identity, one source); the selection here is `--lang`, the same
#          switch `972`'s generic families and `968`'s default voice follow, so the locale names
#          one set of OS-configured answers and not two.
#
# Veil-authored `9NN` numbering: `971` has no `071` to correct; `950`-`999` are Veil-authored with
# no counterpart.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Where the host keyboard leaks, and where it is answered from.
#
# `navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap()` travels renderer -> mojo -> `RenderWidgetHostImpl` ->
# `RenderWidgetHostViewAura` -> `aura::WindowTreeHost` -> `ui::GenerateDomKeyboardLayoutMap()`,
# whose Windows implementation asks `ToUnicodeEx` about the *host's* installed layouts. Every
# persona therefore reported the build host's own 48-key US map (measured, 6.22) -- a macOS persona
# that was never near a US keyboard. The rewrite sits at the chrome-path choke point
# (`RenderWidgetHostViewAura::GetKeyboardLayoutMap`): it is where content owns the answer, it is
# the one site every `chrome`-on-Windows `getLayoutMap()` reaches, and content/browser may include
# `components/ungoogled/ungoogled_switches.h` -- `ui/` may not, which is why the equivalent edit in
# `dom_keyboard_layout_map_win.cc` was rejected rather than routed around with a literal.
#
# Gated on the persona: the rewrite runs only when `--fingerprint=<seed>` is present AND `--lang`
# carries a language the table knows (`en`, `zh`, `de`, `fr`, `ru`). No seed means no claim, and
# the host map is the truth; a claimed language with no table falls through rather than guessing,
# because a wrong map is a stronger tell than a stale one.
#
# The application substitutes **values only**. Which physical keys a machine reports (this host's
# 102nd key, measured as `IntlBackslash: "\\"`) is hardware, not locale, so the key *set* stays
# the host's and the persona neither inserts a key its machine lacks nor deletes one it has --
# the same one-identity split as everything else in this stage: values follow the claim, hardware
# follows the host. Control keys (Shift, Enter, the arrows) are identical across these layouts and
# stay host too. On the committed persona case -- an en-US claim on this US host -- the map is
# therefore leaf-identical, which is the honest reading: the claim already agreed with the host.
# The movement a German claim buys is measured in 6.22.
#
# Assertion 2 holds by construction: the map is answered once per call from one browser-process
# map, so the main frame, a cross-site iframe and a Worker read the same value.


